> I think this needs to change. That's the tool trying to be a daemon. Now
> that we have a daemon in place, it doesn't need an internal loop. It
> looks for new input, processes it, and exits.

Patch 2 does this. lab-process-mail fetches, answers the oldest
message, and exits; with an empty inbox it exits 0 having done
nothing. The daemon runs it again at once while the inbox still has
mail, by asking notmuch, and sleeps only once it is empty, so a batch
is still answered back to back.

This fits the batch idea in "[RFC] processing the entire inbox at
once" [2] too: if one run takes a batch instead of one message, the
daemon does not change.

> This gets installed as a package, right? So I should be able to:
>
>     lab-process-maild start
>
> or stop of course.

Yes. It ships in the lab-process-mail package beside lab-process-mail,
so just install puts it in ~/local/bin. It takes start, stop, and
status. start runs it under daemon(8) for you. stop lets the pass in
progress finish and then exits, so a reply is never left half sent.
The tests drive the real daemon(8) through start, status, and stop.

> Also I'm inclined to have it clear the env. So there should be a
> ~etc/lab-process-maild.conf that lets me set up the env... hopefully it
> can do that.

It can. start keeps only HOME, USER, LOGNAME, and a PATH that begins
with the package's bin/. The conf is sh, and every variable it sets is
exported to lab-process-mail, so yours would be something like:

    LAB_BASE_BRANCH='b/dev@upstream'
    NOTIFY_URL='https://ntfy.sh/your-topic'

If claude needs anything else from your environment to log in, such as
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, it goes in the conf too.

> I want to make the files to go ~/log/ and ~/var/run/ - not sure if
> that's possible. That might need to be part of config. I think package
> manifests have LOCALBASE or root base... though it would be cool if it
> worked in both root and user cases without a config file.

It works for both without a config file. The package keeps its prefix
of /, so pkg -r decides the root, and the daemon finds that root as the
parent of its own bin/, the same way lab-process-mail finds its prompt.
Under that root it uses:

    etc/lab-process-maild.conf
    var/log/lab-process-maild.log
    var/run/lab-process-maild.pid

With just install the root is ~/local, so the files are
~/local/etc/lab-process-maild.conf, ~/local/var/log/, and
~/local/var/run/. A system install puts them in /etc, /var/log, and
/var/run. That is not quite ~/log and ~/var/run. Getting those exactly
would mean installing with pkg -r ~ instead of ~/local, or a setting
in the conf for the two paths. I kept it to the root so there is
nothing to configure; tell me if you want ~/log as written.

The log is opened with daemon -H, so newsyslog can rotate it.

> File a bug report to the list e.g. [BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck
>
> Describe the condition, diagnosis, and possible fixes.

Filed as "[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [3]. While writing it I
found a second silent failure. When claude itself exits non-zero, for
example on a usage limit, set -e ends lab-process-mail before the
inbox check, so no stuck notice goes out either. The report weighs
four fixes. I recommend distinct exit statuses, so the daemon retries
a transient failure and still stops on a stuck message, together with
a notice on every failure. Until one of those lands, the daemon stops
on any failure, as v1 did.

